.TH ZFORTH 1 "2025-08-15" "zForth" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zforth \- small, embeddable Forth interpreter and compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zforth
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zForth
is a small Forth interpreter and compiler written in ANSI C, intended as a
lightweight scripting language for extending applications on resource
constrained systems.
.PP
The compiler and virtual machine state is kept in a single context struct, so
multiple independent instances may run in parallel. The dictionary is stored in
variable length cells, and the virtual machine performs boundary checks on all
memory and stack accesses instead of touching host memory directly.
.PP
Each
.I file
given on the command line is read and evaluated in order. When no files are
given, or after all files have been evaluated,
.B zforth
reads from standard input. If standard input is a terminal, and
.B zforth
was built with readline support, line editing and history are available.
.PP
.B zforth
starts with an empty dictionary containing only the primitives. The standard
library shipped in
.I %%DATADIR%%
is not loaded automatically; source
.I core.zf
first to get the basic words and control structures:
.PP
.RS
.EX
zforth %%DATADIR%%/core.zf
.EE
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Show a short usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-t
Enable tracing. Every word executed by the virtual machine is written to
standard error. This is verbose and slow, but useful when debugging.
.TP
.BI \-l " FILE"
Load a previously saved dictionary image from
.IR FILE .
The image must have been produced by the same
.B zforth
build, since the dictionary layout depends on the compile time configuration in
.IR zfconf.h .
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet. Suppress the banner and the interactive prompt.
.SH "FORTH WORDS"
The interpreter reaches the host through the
.B sys
word, which pops a system call number off the data stack. Seven calls are
implemented, and
.I core.zf
gives each of them a name:
.TP
.B 0 sys \fR\(em\fP emit
Write the character on top of the data stack to standard output.
.TP
.B 1 sys \fR\(em\fP .
Print the value on top of the data stack.
.TP
.B 2 sys \fR\(em\fP tell
Write a counted string from dictionary memory to standard output.
.TP
.B 128 sys \fR\(em\fP quit
Exit the interpreter.
.TP
.B 129 sys \fR\(em\fP sin
Replace the value on top of the data stack with its sine.
.TP
.B 130 sys \fR\(em\fP include
Read the next word as a file name and evaluate that file.
.TP
.B 131 sys \fR\(em\fP save
Write the current dictionary to
.I zforth.save
in the current directory.
.PP
Those names come from
.I core.zf
alone. Without it loaded, only the primitives compiled into the kernel exist, so
even
.B .
is undefined and the raw
.B 1 sys
must be used instead.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I %%DATADIR%%/core.zf
Basic words and control structures. Almost everything else depends on it.
.TP
.I %%DATADIR%%/dict.zf
Words for searching and listing the dictionary.
.TP
.I %%DATADIR%%/memaccess.zf
Typed memory access words.
.TP
.I %%DATADIR%%/misc.zf
Assorted words that have no better home.
.TP
.I %%DATADIR%%/mandel.zf
ASCII Mandelbrot demo. Requires a floating point
.IR zf_cell ,
which is the default in this build.
.TP
.I ./zforth.save
Dictionary image written by the
.B save
word.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.B zforth
exits 0 on success, and 1 if a file given on the command line could not be
evaluated.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start an interactive session with the standard library loaded:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ zforth %%DATADIR%%/core.zf
.EE
.RE
.PP
Evaluate a script and exit:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ echo ': double 2 * ; 21 double . cr' | zforth \-q %%DATADIR%%/core.zf
42
.EE
.RE
.PP
Trace execution without the standard library, printing through the raw
system call:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ echo '1 2 + 1 sys' | zforth \-t \-q
.EE
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
The zForth manual in
.IR %%DOCDIR%% ,
and
.BR pkg-config (1)
for building against the embeddable library
.RB ( pkg-config
.BR zforth ).
.SH AUTHOR
zForth was written by Ico Doornekamp <zforth@zevv.nl>.
